<Project>

  <!--
    Dependency pinning.

    With RestorePackagesWithLockFile enabled, every project writes a
    packages.lock.json recording the exact resolved version and content hash of
    every direct and transitive package. Those files are committed, so:

      * a fresh clone resolves byte-identical packages to a clone by anyone
        else, instead of whatever nuget.org happens to serve that day;
      * the locked restore mode used by CI fails loudly if the lock files and
        the project files ever disagree, rather than silently resolving
        something new.

    One thing to know before changing runtime identifiers: a restore is
    graph-sensitive. If a project is restored once with a runtime identifier and
    once without one, NuGet writes two different graphs into the same lock file,
    and whichever run happens second breaks the other: the installer's
    self-contained win-x64 publish used to leave a lock file that a plain locked
    restore then rejected with NU1004.

    Ferrolite.UI and Ferrolite.Core therefore declare a RuntimeIdentifiers value
    of win-x64 in their project files, so every restore computes the same graph
    no matter which command triggers it. Keep those declarations in sync with
    the self-contained publish that produces a release (the staging script in
    the project repository's installer folder).

    After deliberately changing a package version, regenerate the lock files
    with a forced restore (see BUILD.md) and commit the result.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>true</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
